# port-patches.sh: glm/series-r5 @ e1352d709873 over glm/zig-engine-base @ 0db3e2f6652b (commits b42a552e7693..b54a7d4048bb); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
core prompt cache + lane host: a request keeps its conversation when it continues it without its own turn state (port of GLM 0111)

v1.0.7's store gave each kept state the conversation of the longest kept state
its prompt extends, else a new one, so a request that continued a conversation
without resuming one of its own turn states started a new conversation: a
client that rewinds before the kept turns (an edit, a regenerate, a tool loop
re-sent: GLM issue #104), a state read back from disk, or a conversation whose
states were dropped. TF_GLM_KEEP_PER_CHAT then counted one conversation as
several.

- Store.join decides a request's conversation once, at its lookup: a hit on
  one of a conversation's own turn states keeps that conversation; otherwise
  (a shared cut, a state read back from disk, nothing) the most recently
  used own state whose tokens repeat the prompt through its conversation's
  first prompt and the first 32 tokens of the reply to it (ids no other
  conversation wrote) names the conversation it continues; else a new one.
  draft: false requests never match. Each conversation's first prompt length
  is remembered (roots), forgotten past 4 x max(16, kept states, the
  recipe's entry cap) for conversations no kept state is in.
- The lane host joins at admission and keeps every state of the request in
  its conversation (keepIn), shared cuts included, as Python 0089/0111 do.
- Counted at the prefill: /health and /metrics carry chats_continued and
  chats_continued_prefill_tokens (the GLM recipe's, with a prompt cache
  only, so a default server's /health is byte-identical); rank 0 logs a
  continued request that prefills 4,096 tokens or more, with where the
  prompt left the kept state and what it resumed from.

Switch: none; chat ids drive only the recipe's eviction policy (per_chat, the
entry cap) and /health's kept_mix, never a reply. Tests: the Python fork's
chat_continued_check.py cases translated (prompt_cache_chat_test.zig), the
#104 rewind through real passes, the counters and the log line, the lane host
over five turns of two conversations behind one kept system-prompt cut, and
the /health and /metrics counters.

Measured on the pair (g1, round 1's image, prompt cache on: KV_POOL_GIB=4,
PROMPT_CACHE_GIB=8): exact 39/39 identical to the base engine, 16 cases
resumed from kept states (up to 7,936 cached tokens); a shared 28k-token
system prompt fanned out to 8 requests filled once (prefix_fanout.py
--expect-one-fill: 17-21 s against ~118 s with the cache off, replies equal
the base's).

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo); GLM patch 0111,
MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold#128.
Apply from the checkout root with: git apply 0017-zig-core-chat-continued.patch

